INSERT INTO public.resources (
  title, description, meta_description, url, content_type, content, slug, seo_tags,
  category, reading_minutes, published_at, is_published, featured, access_tier, thumbnail_url
)
VALUES
(
  'How to Improve IELTS Listening from Band 6 to 7: A 4-Week Plan',
  'A focused four-week IELTS Listening plan that turns missed answers into a practical error log, targeted drills, and reliable timed-test habits.',
  'Improve IELTS Listening from Band 6 to 7 with a four-week error-log plan for focus, distractors, spelling, and realistic timed mock tests at home.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/improve-ielts-listening-band-6-to-7-plan',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>Moving from IELTS Listening Band 5.5 or 6 to Band 7 is rarely about finding one secret technique. The more useful question is: <strong>which answers are you losing, and why?</strong> A recent learner with one month left before a university deadline asked how to close exactly this score gap. Other candidates described strong everyday English but inconsistent Listening results under test pressure.</p>
  <p>The plan below replaces random practice with a four-week feedback loop. You will classify each error, drill the cause, and then prove the improvement in full tests.</p>

  <blockquote><p>Do not count only the wrong answers. Count the behaviour that produced them: lost focus, missed paraphrase, distractor, spelling, word-limit error, or a guess made too late.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>Start with the real score gap</h2>
  <p>The official IELTS scoring guide says Listening has 40 questions and each correct answer earns one mark. Raw marks are converted to whole and half bands. IELTS gives 23 correct answers as the average guide for Band 6 and 30 for Band 7, but the precise boundary can vary slightly between test versions.</p>
  <p>That means “I need Band 7” is not yet a practice target. Complete two official-style Listening tests under timed conditions and record the raw result by section and question type. If you score 25 and 27, your job is to recover several repeatable marks—not to rebuild all of your English in four weeks.</p>

  <h2>Build a Listening error log</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Error code</th><th scope="col">What happened</th><th scope="col">Best next drill</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>F</td><td>You lost focus and missed where the recording moved.</td><td>Replay from 20 seconds earlier and mark the phrase that signalled the next answer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>P</td><td>You heard the idea but missed its paraphrase.</td><td>Write the question phrase beside the different phrase used in the audio.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>D</td><td>You chose an option that was mentioned, then rejected or changed.</td><td>Underline contrast signals such as <em>but</em>, <em>however</em>, and <em>actually</em>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>S</td><td>Your meaning was correct but spelling, plural, or grammar was wrong.</td><td>Dictate the answer sentence and check the exact required form.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>I</td><td>You misread the instruction or word limit.</td><td>Rewrite the instruction as a short rule before replaying the item.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>R</td><td>You were still thinking about one answer when the recording advanced.</td><td>Practise a five-second stop-loss: guess, mark it, and rejoin immediately.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>After each test, add one code beside every missed item. At the end of the week, total the codes. Your largest category decides the next drill. This prevents you from spending hours on vocabulary when the real problem is recovery, or repeating full mocks when five marks disappear through spelling.</p>

  <h2>Week 1: diagnose and repair attention</h2>
  <p>Complete two sections at a time rather than a full test every day. Before listening, preview the questions and identify what each blank or option asks for. During review, replay only the evidence zone, then check the transcript. Find the exact words that moved the conversation from the tempting answer to the correct one.</p>
  <p>IELTS states that the Listening recording is heard once and that answers follow the order of information in the recording. Train yourself to notice forward movement. If one answer disappears, your priority is the next question—not a perfect reconstruction of the previous sentence.</p>

  <h2>Week 2: target your two largest error types</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For distractors:</strong> compare the options before the audio and circle the one or two words that make each meaning different.</li>
    <li><strong>For paraphrase:</strong> build pairs from real mistakes, such as “postpone” and “move to a later date.” Review meanings, not isolated synonyms.</li>
    <li><strong>For spelling and form:</strong> run five-minute dictations and check articles, plurals, numbers, dates, and compound words.</li>
    <li><strong>For lost focus:</strong> practise short sections without pausing, then write a one-line summary of what each speaker decided.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If map questions are one of your weak areas, use the <a href="/blog/ielts-listening-map-labelling-strategy">predict-track-answer map strategy</a> rather than treating every Listening task the same way.</p>

  <h2>Week 3: combine accuracy with test pace</h2>
  <p>Return to full sections and then full tests. Use headphones and the same answer-entry method you expect on test day. Do not pause, change the speed, or check the transcript until the section ends. After review, repeat only the missed sequence once without stopping. The goal is to carry a corrected behaviour into normal pace.</p>
  <aside><p><strong>Decision rule:</strong> if full-test scores are not improving but one error code is shrinking, keep the targeted drill for three more sessions. If the same code still dominates, change the drill—not the target.</p></aside>

  <h2>Week 4: prove consistency and taper</h2>
  <p>Complete three realistic tests on separate days. Record the raw score, error-code totals, and the point at which concentration dropped. Your readiness signal is not one lucky Band 7 estimate. It is a stable range with no single error pattern repeatedly costing several answers.</p>
  <p>In the final two days, review the error log and do one or two short sections. Avoid exhausting yourself with repeated full tests. Check your centre instructions, equipment expectations, and test-day timing.</p>

  <h2>Your four-week checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>I have two baseline scores and know my largest error categories.</li>
    <li>I can identify the audio evidence for every corrected answer.</li>
    <li>I recover quickly instead of carrying one missed answer into the next.</li>
    <li>I check word limits, spelling, and singular or plural form.</li>
    <li>My last three realistic tests show a stable range.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Final action: run a diagnostic today</h2>
  <p>Start one timed test in the <a href="/listening">IELTS Listening practice area</a> or choose a complete set from <a href="/cambridge-tests">Cambridge IELTS practice</a>. Create the six-code error log before you check the answers. If screen workflow also costs you marks, pair this plan with the <a href="/blog/ielts-computer-test-typing-screen-strategy">7-day computer-test guide</a>.</p>
  <p>Official facts in this guide were checked on 19 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/your-results/ielts-scoring-in-detail" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">scoring explanation</a> and <a href="https://ielts.org/organisations/ielts-for-organisations/test-types/ielts-academic-test/academic-test-format-in-detail" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">test-format details</a>.</p>
  $post$,
  'improve-ielts-listening-band-6-to-7-plan',
  ARRAY['improve IELTS Listening score', 'IELTS Listening Band 6 to 7', 'IELTS Listening study plan', 'IELTS Listening error log', 'IELTS Listening practice', 'IELTS Listening tips'],
  'Listening', 5, '2026-07-19T08:00:00Z'::timestamptz, false, false, 'free',
  '/blog-images/ielts-listening-band-6-to-7-plan.webp'
),
(
  'IELTS Speaking Band 5 vs 6 vs 7: What Changes?',
  'Compare how IELTS Speaking performance develops from Band 5 to 6 to 7, then use a practical Part 3 framework to improve your recorded answers.',
  'Compare IELTS Speaking Bands 5, 6, and 7 across fluency, vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation, then practise stronger Part 3 answers with confidence.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-speaking-band-5-vs-6-vs-7',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>Learners often ask for Band 5, 6, and 7 IELTS Speaking samples because a single “good answer” does not show what actually changes between levels. A recent candidate specifically wanted recordings or transcripts that reveal those differences. Others felt their Part 3 answers were too short or difficult to develop, even when their everyday English felt natural.</p>
  <p>The important point is that IELTS does not award a band from one memorised answer. Examiners assess your performance across the test using four equally weighted criteria: fluency and coherence, lexical resource, grammatical range and accuracy, and pronunciation.</p>

  <blockquote><p>The move from Band 5 to 7 is not “use fancier words.” It is greater control: ideas are easier to follow, vocabulary is more flexible, complex sentences work more reliably, and pronunciation supports clear communication.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>The four official criteria</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">What to listen for in your recording</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Fluency and coherence</td><td>Can you keep going, connect ideas logically, and avoid long content-search pauses?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lexical resource</td><td>Can you choose precise everyday words, paraphrase, and discuss unfamiliar topics without collapsing?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Grammatical range and accuracy</td><td>Can you mix simple and complex structures while keeping enough sentences error-free?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pronunciation</td><td>Can a listener follow you without strain, with useful stress, rhythm, and intonation?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Accent is not a fifth criterion. The goal is intelligibility and control, not copying a British or American voice.</p>

  <h2>Band 5 vs 6 vs 7 in plain English</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Around Band 5</th><th scope="col">Around Band 6</th><th scope="col">Around Band 7</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Flow</td><td>Simple ideas continue, but complex answers bring repetition, slow speech, or noticeable searching.</td><td>You can speak at length, though hesitation or self-correction sometimes weakens coherence.</td><td>You produce longer turns readily; some hesitation remains, but it does not normally damage the message.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Vocabulary</td><td>Vocabulary works for familiar topics but has limited flexibility; paraphrase is uneven.</td><td>You have enough range to discuss topics at length and usually paraphrase successfully.</td><td>You use vocabulary flexibly across topics, including some less common or idiomatic items with awareness of style.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Grammar</td><td>Basic forms are reasonably controlled, while complex structures are limited and error-prone.</td><td>You use both simple and complex forms, but errors in complex sentences are frequent.</td><td>You use a range of complex structures with flexibility, and many sentences are error-free.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pronunciation</td><td>Speech is generally understandable, but mispronunciation can make the listener work.</td><td>Pronunciation features are used with mixed control; meaning is generally clear.</td><td>You use a wider range of pronunciation features with enough control to stay easy to understand.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>These are practical paraphrases of the public band descriptors, not a replacement for them. A candidate does not need every Band 7 feature in every sentence; the official descriptor explains that the score reflects the overall fit across positive features.</p>

  <h2>One Part 3 question at three levels</h2>
  <p>Imagine the examiner asks: <em>Why do some people prefer working from home?</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Developing response:</strong> “Because it is comfortable. People do not travel, and they have more time.” The answer is relevant but stops before explaining the consequence.</li>
    <li><strong>More controlled response:</strong> “One reason is that people can avoid a long commute, so they have more time and energy for work. For example, someone who normally travels an hour each way can use part of that time to plan the day.” The idea, reason, and example connect clearly.</li>
    <li><strong>Flexible response:</strong> “Avoiding the commute is a major attraction, particularly in large cities. It can improve concentration because employees begin the day with more energy; however, that benefit depends on having a quiet space at home.” The response develops, qualifies, and contrasts without sounding rehearsed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The third response is not stronger because it is longer. It gives the claim a reason, consequence, and sensible limit.</p>

  <h2>Use the A-R-E-C framework for Part 3</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Answer:</strong> give your position directly.</li>
    <li><strong>Reason:</strong> explain why it is true.</li>
    <li><strong>Example:</strong> make the reasoning concrete.</li>
    <li><strong>Contrast:</strong> when useful, show another group, time, place, or limitation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>You do not need all four moves every time. Use Answer + Reason for a simple question; add Example or Contrast when the examiner invites deeper analysis. This keeps the structure flexible and conversational.</p>

  <h2>If you cannot think of an idea</h2>
  <p>Part 3 asks you to discuss issues in a general and more abstract way. It tests your ability to explain opinions, analyse, discuss, and speculate—not your expertise in the topic. Choose a reasonable position you can explain. You may compare older and younger people, cities and rural areas, past and present, individuals and governments, or short-term and long-term effects.</p>
  <aside><p><strong>Useful self-check:</strong> after recording, underline the exact sentence that answers the question. If you cannot find it, improve directness before adding advanced vocabulary.</p></aside>

  <h2>A 15-minute Band 6-to-7 drill</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Record three Part 3 answers without restarting.</li>
    <li>Transcribe one answer exactly, including repetitions and unfinished sentences.</li>
    <li>Mark A, R, E, and C beside the moves you actually used.</li>
    <li>Choose one change only: clearer position, stronger reason, useful example, or cleaner complex sentence.</li>
    <li>Record the answer again, then compare clarity rather than word count.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Final action: record one Part 3 set</h2>
  <p>Open the <a href="/speaking">IELTS Speaking practice area</a> and complete one set without memorising a script. Review it against all four criteria, then repeat only the weakest answer using A-R-E-C. If your Part 2 answers also end early, use the <a href="/blog/ielts-speaking-part-2-two-minute-answer">two-minute Part 2 framework</a>. You can explore how skill scores affect a target overall result with the <a href="/ielts-band-score-calculator">IELTS band score calculator</a>.</p>
  <p>Official claims were checked on 19 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/test-types/ielts-academic-test/ielts-academic-format-speaking" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speaking test format</a> and current <a href="https://ielts.org/cdn/ielts-guides/ielts-speaking-band-descriptors.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speaking band descriptors</a>.</p>
  $post$,
  'ielts-speaking-band-5-vs-6-vs-7',
  ARRAY['IELTS Speaking Band 5 vs 6 vs 7', 'IELTS Speaking band descriptors', 'IELTS Speaking Band 7', 'IELTS Speaking Part 3 answers', 'improve IELTS Speaking', 'IELTS Speaking practice'],
  'Speaking', 5, '2026-07-19T09:00:00Z'::timestamptz, false, false, 'free',
  '/blog-images/ielts-speaking-band-5-6-7-comparison.webp'
),
(
  'IELTS Reading Matching Headings: Find the Main Idea Faster',
  'A practical main-idea method for IELTS Reading matching headings, with elimination rules for similar options, detail traps, and difficult paragraphs.',
  'Solve IELTS Reading matching headings faster with a main-idea map, elimination rules for similar options, and a focused practice checklist today.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-reading-matching-headings-main-idea',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>Matching headings can make a Reading section feel slow even when you understand most of the vocabulary. Recent learners described two related problems: paragraphs contain so much information that the central idea is hard to name, and several headings seem plausible because they repeat details from the text.</p>
  <p>The task becomes more reliable when you stop hunting for matching words and instead write a short <strong>main-idea map</strong> for each paragraph.</p>

  <blockquote><p>A correct heading covers the paragraph’s controlling idea. A wrong heading often describes one true detail that is too small to represent the paragraph.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>What matching headings actually tests</h2>
  <p>IELTS defines a heading as a summary of the main idea of a paragraph or section. The official format notes that there are more headings than paragraphs, some headings are unused, and no heading can be used more than once. The task tests whether you can distinguish the main idea from supporting information.</p>
  <p>This is different from matching information. Matching information sends you to a specific fact, example, reason, or explanation. Matching headings asks what the whole paragraph is doing.</p>

  <h2>Use the Topic + Point + Purpose map</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">What to write</th><th scope="col">Example note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Topic</td><td>What broad subject appears throughout the paragraph?</td><td>urban cycling</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Point</td><td>What does the writer say about that topic?</td><td>increased after safer lanes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Why is this paragraph here: problem, cause, change, contrast, example, or solution?</td><td>explains cause of growth</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Your final paragraph note could be: “safer lanes caused cycling growth.” That short sentence is easier to compare with ten headings than the full paragraph is.</p>

  <h2>Step 1: compare the headings before the text</h2>
  <p>Read the complete headings and underline the words that make similar options different. For example, “the cost of a new system,” “opposition to a new system,” and “unexpected benefits of a new system” share the same topic. The deciding meaning is cost, opposition, or benefits.</p>
  <p>Paraphrase difficult headings in simpler language. Do not predict a paragraph from one keyword; the text may use the same word for a supporting example.</p>

  <h2>Step 2: read for the paragraph’s job</h2>
  <p>Start with the first sentence, then skim the rest and check the final sentence. Topic sentences often appear near the beginning, but the central idea may become clear later through a contrast such as <em>however</em> or a concluding result such as <em>therefore</em>. Ask what most of the sentences support.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>If four sentences explain why a change happened and one gives a date, the paragraph is about the cause—not the date.</li>
    <li>If the first half presents an old belief and the second half overturns it, the change of view is probably central.</li>
    <li>If several examples illustrate one problem, choose the problem rather than the most memorable example.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 3: eliminate detail traps</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Trap</th><th scope="col">Why it feels right</th><th scope="col">Why it is wrong</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Repeated-word trap</td><td>The heading and paragraph share an obvious noun.</td><td>The noun appears only in an example, not the main claim.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Half-right heading</td><td>It matches the paragraph’s topic.</td><td>It gives the wrong point about that topic.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Too-narrow heading</td><td>It accurately describes one sentence.</td><td>It fails to cover the rest of the paragraph.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Too-broad heading</td><td>It could describe several paragraphs.</td><td>It misses the specific purpose of this one.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Opposite-direction trap</td><td>It uses the right subject and vocabulary.</td><td>It reverses a cause, result, benefit, or criticism.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Step 4: use elimination without forcing an answer</h2>
  <p>Match the clearest paragraph first and cross out that heading. If two options remain plausible, write your Topic + Point + Purpose note and move on. A later match may eliminate one option. Returning with fewer choices is usually faster than rereading the same difficult paragraph immediately.</p>
  <p>Because official IELTS guidance confirms that wrong answers are not penalised, never leave a heading blank. Make the best evidence-based choice before time expires.</p>

  <h2>A worked decision example</h2>
  <p>Suppose a paragraph begins with early enthusiasm for electric buses, then explains that high battery costs slowed adoption, and ends by describing government subsidies that restarted purchases. Three headings might mention enthusiasm, cost barriers, or government action.</p>
  <p>The Topic is electric-bus adoption. The Point is that subsidies reversed a slowdown. The Purpose is to explain a solution and renewed change. A heading about government support reviving adoption covers the full movement; a heading about battery cost describes only the middle obstacle.</p>

  <aside><p><strong>Five-second test:</strong> can you explain how at least two sentences support the heading? If only one sentence fits, the option is probably a detail trap.</p></aside>

  <h2>A focused 20-minute practice routine</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose one passage with matching headings and set a strict timer.</li>
    <li>Write a six-to-ten-word Topic + Point + Purpose note for each required paragraph.</li>
    <li>Record why every rejected heading is too narrow, too broad, or wrong in direction.</li>
    <li>Check answers, then compare your paragraph notes with the actual main ideas.</li>
    <li>Redo only the missed paragraphs the next day without looking at your first choices.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Final action: map one passage</h2>
  <p>Open the <a href="/reading">IELTS Reading practice area</a> and create a main-idea map before selecting headings. Pair the method with the <a href="/blog/ielts-reading-time-management-computer-strategy">60-minute Reading strategy</a> so one difficult paragraph cannot consume the section. If your errors are mainly evidence decisions rather than headings, practise the <a href="/blog/ielts-reading-true-false-not-given-strategy">True, False, Not Given evidence method</a>.</p>
  <p>Official claims were checked on 19 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/test-types/ielts-academic-test/ielts-academic-format-reading" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Academic Reading format</a> and its official <a href="https://ielts.org/news-and-insights/how-to-prepare-learners-for-matching-headings-task-on-the-ielts-reading-test" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">matching-headings strategy</a>.</p>
  $post$,
  'ielts-reading-matching-headings-main-idea',
  ARRAY['IELTS Reading matching headings', 'matching headings IELTS strategy', 'IELTS Reading main idea', 'IELTS heading matching tips', 'IELTS Reading question types', 'IELTS Reading practice'],
  'Reading', 5, '2026-07-19T10:00:00Z'::timestamptz, false, false, 'free',
  '/blog-images/ielts-reading-matching-headings-main-idea.webp'
)
