INSERT INTO public.resources (
  title, description, meta_description, url, content_type, content, slug, seo_tags,
  category, reading_minutes, published_at, is_published, featured, access_tier, thumbnail_url
)
VALUES
(
  'IELTS Writing Coherence and Cohesion: Band 6 to 7',
  'A practical paragraph-flow method for IELTS Writing candidates who have enough ideas but struggle to connect, sequence, and develop them clearly.',
  'Move IELTS Writing coherence and cohesion from Band 6 to 7 with a paragraph-flow method, reference-word checks, and a focused revision drill.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-writing-coherence-cohesion-band-7',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>You finish a Task 2 essay with a minute left, reread the first body paragraph, and realise every sentence is individually understandable—but the paragraph still feels jumpy. Adding <em>moreover</em> or <em>therefore</em> will not repair a missing relationship between the ideas.</p>
  <p>This is the problem behind many requests from learners around Band 6 or 6.5: they can produce relevant points, yet struggle to phrase and connect them under time pressure. The useful fix is not a longer list of linkers. It is making the logic visible before choosing the language that carries it.</p>

  <blockquote><p>Coherence is the route your argument follows. Cohesion is the language that helps the reader stay on that route.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>What changes from Band 6 to Band 7?</h2>
  <p>IELTS assesses Writing through four criteria, and coherence and cohesion is one of them. Official preparation guidance distinguishes clear overall progression at Band 6 from logical organisation and clear progression throughout the response at Band 7. That difference is easy to miss: an essay may have an introduction, body paragraphs, and a conclusion, but still lose its thread inside a paragraph.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Band 6 risk</th><th scope="col">Band 7 direction</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Paragraph purpose</td><td>The paragraph is relevant, but two competing ideas share the space.</td><td>One central topic controls the paragraph.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Progression</td><td>The essay moves forward overall, with a few abrupt or unclear steps.</td><td>Each sentence has a clear job in developing the point.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cohesive devices</td><td>Linking can sound mechanical or repetitive.</td><td>Connectors, reference words, and sentence structure are used flexibly and appropriately.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Referencing</td><td>Words such as <em>this</em> or <em>they</em> sometimes have an unclear target.</td><td>The reader can identify exactly what each reference points to.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>You do not need perfect grammar or a connector in every sentence. You need a sequence the reader can follow without guessing.</p>

  <h2>Use the Point–Why–Result–Proof chain</h2>
  <p>Before writing a body paragraph, make four short notes. They are not a rigid template; they are a logic check.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Point:</strong> What is the one claim this paragraph will make?</li>
    <li><strong>Why:</strong> What mechanism or reason makes the claim true?</li>
    <li><strong>Result:</strong> What consequence follows from that reason?</li>
    <li><strong>Proof:</strong> What realistic example or situation makes the chain concrete?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Suppose the question asks whether cities should charge drivers to enter crowded centres. Your notes might be: <em>charge discourages optional trips → fewer cars at peak time → buses move faster → commuters choose public transport when journey times become reliable</em>.</p>
  <p>Now the paragraph has movement. You can decide whether a connector is needed after you know the relationship.</p>

  <h2>Choose the relationship before the linking word</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Relationship</th><th scope="col">Question to ask</th><th scope="col">Natural language options</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cause</td><td>Why does this happen?</td><td>because, since, as a result of</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Result</td><td>What follows?</td><td>so, therefore, this means that</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Contrast</td><td>What is the limit or other side?</td><td>however, although, while</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Example</td><td>What makes the point concrete?</td><td>for example, such as, a clear case is</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reference</td><td>Can I avoid repeating the whole noun phrase?</td><td>this approach, such a policy, these changes</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Official IELTS guidance warns against forcing a linking word into every sentence. Sometimes sentence order, a pronoun, or a repeated key concept creates a smoother connection than another formal connector.</p>

  <h2>A worked paragraph repair</h2>
  <p><strong>Jumpy version:</strong> “Traffic charges reduce congestion. Moreover, public transport can be unreliable. Therefore, people use cars. For example, buses are late.”</p>
  <p>The vocabulary is not the main problem. The relationships contradict one another: the first sentence supports charging, then the paragraph moves to unreliable buses without explaining whether that is a cause, a limitation, or a solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Repaired logic:</strong> “Charging drivers can reduce congestion by discouraging optional journeys at peak times. With fewer cars on central roads, buses are more likely to keep to their schedules. This improvement can make public transport a realistic alternative for commuters who currently drive because they fear arriving late.”</p>
  <p>The repaired version uses ordinary vocabulary. It is clearer because each sentence grows from the previous one: policy, mechanism, result, and practical consequence.</p>

  <aside><p><strong>Two-sentence test:</strong> cover every linker and read only the nouns and verbs. If the logic disappears, the paragraph needs better development—not more connective phrases.</p></aside>

  <h2>Run a four-colour revision</h2>
  <p>After one timed essay, mark the paragraph by function. You can use colours on paper or simple labels in a document.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>P:</strong> the central point;</li>
    <li><strong>W:</strong> the reason or mechanism;</li>
    <li><strong>R:</strong> the result or implication;</li>
    <li><strong>E:</strong> the example or evidence.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If a paragraph contains two P labels, split or remove one idea. If it has a Point and Example but no Why, add the missing explanation. If every sentence begins with a memorised linker, delete half and see whether sentence order can carry the meaning.</p>

  <h2>A 20-minute coherence drill</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Spend three minutes making Point–Why–Result–Proof notes for two body paragraphs.</li>
    <li>Write one paragraph in eight minutes without consulting a linker list.</li>
    <li>Use the four labels to identify the job of each sentence.</li>
    <li>Circle every pronoun or reference phrase and draw an arrow to its exact target.</li>
    <li>Rewrite only the weakest transition, then compare the two versions aloud.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Final action: test one paragraph, not a whole template</h2>
  <p>Open the <a href="/writing">IELTS Writing practice area</a>, choose one Task 2 prompt, and build a single body paragraph with the four-step chain. Use the <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-essay-structure-band-7">Band 7 essay structure guide</a> for the wider essay plan, or the <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-1-time-management-20-minutes">Task 1 timing guide</a> if your paragraph decisions are costing time in the first task.</p>
  <p>Official scoring and preparation claims were checked on 20 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/preparation-resources/writing-test-resources" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Writing test preparation resources</a> and <a href="https://ielts.org/news-and-insights/how-to-teach-paragraph-level-cohesion" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">paragraph-level cohesion guidance</a>.</p>
  $post$,
  'ielts-writing-coherence-cohesion-band-7',
  ARRAY['IELTS writing coherence and cohesion', 'IELTS coherence and cohesion Band 7', 'how to connect ideas in IELTS writing', 'IELTS Writing Band 6 to 7', 'IELTS cohesive devices', 'IELTS Task 2 paragraph flow'],
  'Writing', 6, '2026-07-20T08:00:00Z'::timestamptz, false, false, 'free',
  '/blog-images/ielts-writing-coherence-band-7.webp'
),
(
  'IELTS Speaking Band 7 to 8: Grammar and Vocabulary Drills',
  'A focused recording routine for IELTS Speaking learners who are fluent but need greater grammatical control, precise vocabulary, and reliable paraphrasing.',
  'Target IELTS Speaking Band 8 with recording drills for grammar control, precise vocabulary, paraphrasing, and natural correction across Parts 1–3.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-speaking-band-7-to-8-grammar-vocabulary',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>Your answer sounds fluent when you say it, but the transcript tells a different story: repeated adjectives, unfinished complex sentences, and the same small grammar error appearing in every topic. That is a common frustration for a learner already around Band 7. More speaking is useful, but repeating the same patterns at full speed can also make them more automatic.</p>
  <p>A recent learner preparing within days described exactly this split: fluency and pronunciation felt stronger, while grammar and lexical resource kept pulling the result down. This guide turns those two criteria into a short record–tag–repair routine.</p>

  <blockquote><p>Do not chase “Band 8 words.” Build enough control to choose a precise word and enough grammar range to express a more exact relationship.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>Start with what the examiner actually assesses</h2>
  <p>IELTS Speaking is assessed across the whole test using four equally weighted criteria: fluency and coherence, lexical resource, grammatical range and accuracy, and pronunciation. This article focuses on two criteria, but improving them does not guarantee a particular overall band if another area remains weaker.</p>
  <p>In the public Speaking descriptors, Band 7 allows some errors alongside flexible language and many controlled structures. Band 8 requires broader, more reliable control: vocabulary is used readily and precisely, paraphrase is effective, and most sentences are accurate. The practical direction is consistency, not theatrical complexity.</p>

  <h2>Use two review lanes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Lane</th><th scope="col">What to tag in a recording</th><th scope="col">Repair target</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Grammar accuracy</td><td>Subject–verb agreement, articles, tense shifts, plurals, word order.</td><td>One repeated error pattern across several answers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Grammar range</td><td>Only short simple sentences, or complex sentences that repeatedly break.</td><td>One useful relationship: reason, condition, contrast, or relative detail.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Vocabulary precision</td><td>General words such as <em>good</em>, <em>bad</em>, <em>thing</em>, or <em>very</em> hide the exact meaning.</td><td>A familiar, more exact phrase that fits the topic.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Vocabulary flexibility</td><td>You stop when the first word does not come, or repeat the question wording.</td><td>A natural paraphrase or short explanation.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Choose one lane per practice session. Trying to repair fluency, pronunciation, grammar, and vocabulary in the same answer creates too much monitoring and usually makes speech less natural.</p>

  <h2>The record–tag–repair–repeat method</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Record:</strong> answer three questions without restarting or reading a script.</li>
    <li><strong>Tag:</strong> transcribe one answer exactly and mark repeated grammar errors or vague vocabulary.</li>
    <li><strong>Repair:</strong> rewrite only two sentences, preserving your original idea and speaking style.</li>
    <li><strong>Repeat:</strong> answer the question again from one-line notes, not from the corrected transcript.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The final step matters. Reading a perfect sentence proves reading control. Producing the pattern again while thinking proves that it is becoming available in speech.</p>

  <h2>Grammar: build range from meaning</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Meaning you need</th><th scope="col">Structure to practise</th><th scope="col">Speaking example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Give a reason</td><td>because / since clause</td><td>“I prefer studying early because the house is quieter.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Add a condition</td><td>if clause</td><td>“If the journey is short, I usually walk.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Show contrast</td><td>although / while clause</td><td>“Although online classes are convenient, I concentrate better in a classroom.”</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Add precise detail</td><td>relative clause</td><td>“The teacher who influenced me most made difficult ideas feel manageable.”</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Use one structure in three different topics. If accuracy collapses, shorten the sentence and keep the relationship. Range is useful only when the listener can still follow the message.</p>

  <h2>Vocabulary: replace lists with topic webs</h2>
  <p>For one common topic, make a small web with five parts: people, places, actions, problems, and effects. For <em>public transport</em>, that could include <em>commuters</em>, <em>suburbs</em>, <em>transfer between lines</em>, <em>overcrowding</em>, and <em>shorter journey times</em>.</p>
  <p>Then practise paraphrasing one item without searching for a rare synonym:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><em>overcrowding</em> → “too many passengers for the available space”;</li>
    <li><em>reliable</em> → “it arrives when people expect it to”;</li>
    <li><em>commute</em> → “the regular journey between home and work.”</li>
  </ul>
  <p>IELTS includes the ability to express meaning with alternative vocabulary when a particular word is unavailable. A clear explanation is often better evidence of flexibility than a risky “advanced” word.</p>

  <h2>A worked answer upgrade</h2>
  <p>Question: <em>Do people in your city use public transport often?</em></p>
  <p><strong>First version:</strong> “Yes, many people use it because it is good and not expensive. But sometimes it is bad because many people are there.”</p>
  <p><strong>Repaired version:</strong> “Yes, especially during the working week, because buses are cheaper than driving and parking. However, they become overcrowded at peak times, which makes the journey less comfortable.”</p>
  <p>The second answer is not stronger because it is longer. It replaces vague evaluation with precise cause, contrast, and detail. It also uses two controlled complex structures without sounding memorised.</p>

  <aside><p><strong>Correction rule:</strong> correct yourself once when the repair is quick and useful. Rebuilding the whole sentence repeatedly can damage fluency more than a minor error would.</p></aside>

  <h2>A 15-minute daily drill</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Record one Part 1, one Part 2 follow-up, and one Part 3 answer.</li>
    <li>Transcribe the shortest answer.</li>
    <li>Count one grammar pattern and one vague word—not every possible weakness.</li>
    <li>Build two repair sentences and say each three times with different details.</li>
    <li>Re-answer the original question once and stop.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Final action: create one honest transcript</h2>
  <p>Open the <a href="/speaking">IELTS Speaking practice area</a> and record one set without a prepared script. Tag the answer in just one review lane. Compare your evidence with the <a href="/blog/ielts-speaking-band-5-vs-6-vs-7">Band 5, 6, and 7 comparison</a>, and use the <a href="/blog/ielts-speaking-part-2-two-minute-answer">Part 2 two-minute framework</a> if grammar control disappears mainly during longer turns.</p>
  <p>Official scoring claims were checked on 20 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/test-types/ielts-academic-test/ielts-academic-format-speaking" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speaking test format</a> and <a href="https://ielts.org/cdn/ielts-guides/ielts-speaking-key-assessment-criteria.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Speaking assessment criteria</a>.</p>
  $post$,
  'ielts-speaking-band-7-to-8-grammar-vocabulary',
  ARRAY['IELTS Speaking Band 7 to 8', 'IELTS Speaking grammar practice', 'IELTS Speaking vocabulary practice', 'IELTS lexical resource speaking', 'IELTS grammatical range and accuracy', 'improve IELTS Speaking Band 8'],
  'Speaking', 6, '2026-07-20T09:00:00Z'::timestamptz, false, false, 'free',
  '/blog-images/ielts-speaking-grammar-vocabulary-band-8.webp'
),
(
  'Are IELTS Mock Test Scores Accurate? A Readiness Checklist',
  'A decision guide for interpreting changing IELTS mock-test scores, choosing credible practice conditions, and judging readiness from a stable pattern.',
  'Learn when IELTS mock-test scores are useful, why results fluctuate, and how to judge readiness with a three-test consistency and review checklist.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-mock-test-score-readiness-checklist',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>You score 7.5 in Listening on Saturday, 6.5 on Monday, and immediately wonder which result is “real.” Recent IELTS learners have asked whether mock tests are harder or easier than test day, while others report practice ranges that moved in either direction once pressure, concentration, and unfamiliar questions entered the room.</p>
  <p>No honest checklist can convert one unofficial mock into a guaranteed official score. It can help you decide whether the result is useful evidence, a warning about unstable performance, or simply a badly controlled practice session.</p>

  <blockquote><p>A mock score is a measurement from one set of questions under one set of conditions. Read the pattern, not the most exciting number.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>What an official practice score can and cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>IELTS says its official Trial Test can help you understand your current level and experience timed conditions, but the result is not an official IELTS score and cannot be used for academic, immigration, or work purposes. That boundary matters even more for third-party tests whose content, difficulty, or marking process you cannot verify.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">A mock can help you estimate</th><th scope="col">A mock cannot guarantee</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Whether you can finish within the real time limit.</td><td>Your exact official band on a future day.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Which question types repeatedly cost marks.</td><td>That one website is always harder or easier than IELTS.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Whether concentration drops in a full sitting.</td><td>How an institution will use an unofficial score.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Whether several recent results form a stable range.</td><td>Speaking or Writing accuracy without credible assessment criteria and feedback.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Run the five-part credibility check</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Useful evidence</th><th scope="col">Warning sign</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Source</td><td>Official sample material or a test that clearly follows the current IELTS format.</td><td>Unknown authorship, copied questions, or no explanation of format.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Setup</td><td>One sitting, real time limit, no pauses, normal answer-entry method.</td><td>Checking answers during the test or extending time.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scoring</td><td>Listening and Reading use correct answers; Writing and Speaking use the public criteria.</td><td>A single automated number with no criterion-level evidence.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Freshness</td><td>You have not memorised the passage, audio, or answer key.</td><td>Repeated exposure makes the score reflect memory.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Review</td><td>Every lost mark or weak criterion becomes a specific next drill.</td><td>You collect scores but cannot explain what changed.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Use three tests to find a working range</h2>
  <p>The three-test rule below is a study decision aid, not an official IELTS requirement. Take three fresh, comparable mocks on separate days under the same conditions. Record each skill score, the raw Listening and Reading marks, and one note about sleep, interruptions, equipment, or unusual anxiety.</p>
  <p>Then look at the lowest score, the highest score, and the spread between them:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>If all three meet your requirement and the error patterns are small, you have stronger readiness evidence.</li>
    <li>If one high result meets the target but two do not, plan from the lower range rather than the peak.</li>
    <li>If scores swing widely, diagnose conditions and question types before booking based on an average.</li>
    <li>If Reading and Listening are stable but Writing or Speaking estimates jump, seek criterion-level feedback rather than taking more full mocks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A worked readiness decision</h2>
  <p>Imagine a learner needs at least 7.0 in Listening and gets 7.5, 6.5, and 7.0 on three fresh tests. The average may look reassuring, but the range shows that Band 7 is not yet protected. The review notes reveal that most losses came from multiple-choice distractors late in Sections 3 and 4.</p>
  <p>The next step is not a fourth random mock tomorrow. It is two focused distractor sessions, followed by another full test after recovery time. If the new score meets the target and the same error cluster shrinks, the readiness evidence becomes more useful.</p>

  <aside><p><strong>Booking rule:</strong> compare your recent lower-bound performance with every requirement that matters, including minimum skill scores. A strong overall average does not replace a required minimum in one section.</p></aside>

  <h2>Why mock scores fluctuate</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Content fit:</strong> familiar topics reduce cognitive load; unfamiliar ones reveal whether the method transfers.</li>
    <li><strong>Test conditions:</strong> pausing, replaying, checking a phone, or using a different screen changes the task.</li>
    <li><strong>Fatigue:</strong> a full sitting tests attention endurance as well as individual question skills.</li>
    <li><strong>Marking quality:</strong> Reading and Listening have answer keys; Writing and Speaking require judgement across criteria.</li>
    <li><strong>Repetition:</strong> remembered answers can create a score that no longer measures first-attempt performance.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your readiness checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>I used fresh material that reflects the current test format.</li>
    <li>I completed three comparable tests on separate days without pausing.</li>
    <li>I recorded raw marks, band estimates, and conditions—not only the overall score.</li>
    <li>My lower recent scores meet the requirements I actually need.</li>
    <li>I can name the two error patterns most likely to cost marks.</li>
    <li>I have practised the computer or paper workflow I expect on test day.</li>
    <li>My Writing and Speaking review uses criterion-level evidence, not only a predicted band.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Final action: make the next mock count</h2>
  <p>Choose a fresh set in the <a href="/cambridge-tests">IELTS mock-test area</a>, write down the exact conditions before you start, and complete it in one sitting. Use the <a href="/ielts-band-score-calculator">IELTS band score calculator</a> to explore the skill-score combination, then place the result beside two more comparable attempts. The <a href="/blog/30-day-ielts-study-plan-band-7">30-day study plan</a> can turn the weakest pattern into a weekly target, while the <a href="/blog/ielts-computer-test-typing-screen-strategy">computer-test practice guide</a> helps control screen and typing effects.</p>
  <p>Official claims were checked on 20 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/preparation-resources/ielts-trial-test" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Trial Test guidance</a> and <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/preparation-resources/sample-test-questions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official sample-test resources</a>.</p>
  $post$,
  'ielts-mock-test-score-readiness-checklist',
  ARRAY['are IELTS mock test scores accurate', 'IELTS mock test score', 'IELTS test readiness', 'IELTS practice score vs real score', 'how many IELTS mock tests', 'IELTS mock test checklist'],
  'Study Planning', 6, '2026-07-20T10:00:00Z'::timestamptz, false, false, 'free',
  '/blog-images/ielts-mock-test-score-readiness.webp'
)
