VALUES
(
  'IELTS Writing Task 2 Off-Topic? Use the Prompt-Lock Check',
  'A practical prompt-analysis and paragraph-checking method for IELTS writers who overwrite, drift into the wider topic, or answer only part of Task 2.',
  'Stay on topic in IELTS Writing Task 2 with a prompt-lock plan, paragraph relevance checks, and a clear method to stop overwriting under time pressure.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-stay-on-topic',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>You are halfway through the second body paragraph when a worrying thought appears: <em>This sounds intelligent, but is it answering the question?</em> You add another example to make the essay feel safer. Five minutes later, the response is longer, the ending is rushed, and the new material has moved even further from the task.</p>
  <p>This is not mainly a vocabulary problem. It is a control problem. Recent learners have described writing too much, losing the neat shape of the essay, and gradually straying from the topic. The fix is to lock every paragraph to the exact task before you start expanding it.</p>

  <blockquote><p>Your essay is not about everything you know on the subject. It is a direct answer to one specific instruction.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>Why “related” is not the same as “relevant”</h2>
  <p>Official IELTS guidance says Task 2 should address the most relevant issues and answer the task fully. Task response assesses how accurately, appropriately, and relevantly you cover the requirements. IELTS also warns that very long answers can leave less time for checking and may contain ideas that are not directly relevant.</p>
  <p>Imagine the question asks whether governments should fund vocational training <em>instead of</em> university education. A paragraph about the general importance of education is related to the topic. It is not yet relevant to the choice in the question. Relevance appears when the paragraph explains how funding one route rather than the other affects people, employers, or public resources.</p>

  <h2>Build a prompt lock in four boxes</h2>
  <p>Before brainstorming examples, divide the question into four parts. Write only a few words in each box.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Box</th><th scope="col">Question to ask</th><th scope="col">Example note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Topic</td><td>What broad subject is being discussed?</td><td>Public funding for education</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Focus</td><td>Which narrow comparison, cause, or problem matters?</td><td>Vocational training versus university</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Instruction</td><td>What must I do: agree, discuss, explain causes, or propose solutions?</td><td>Give the extent of my agreement</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Position</td><td>What precise answer will remain consistent?</td><td>Prioritise shortages, but keep balanced funding</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>This four-box note is your prompt lock. If an idea does not connect to the focus, instruction, and position, it does not enter the essay—even when the idea sounds impressive.</p>

  <h2>Use the because–therefore relevance test</h2>
  <p>Test each body-paragraph idea with one sentence:</p>
  <p><strong>I believe [position] because [paragraph reason]; therefore [direct effect on the exact question].</strong></p>
  <p>For the example above: “I believe governments should increase vocational funding because some industries face practical skill shortages; therefore targeted courses can connect public spending more directly to unfilled jobs.”</p>
  <p>If the <em>therefore</em> clause returns to the wording of the task, the idea is probably relevant. If it returns only to the broad topic—“therefore education is important”—the paragraph is still too general.</p>

  <h2>A worked drift-and-repair example</h2>
  <p><strong>Drifting version:</strong> “Universities have existed for centuries and teach many interesting subjects. Students also meet people from different backgrounds, which is good for society. Education helps countries develop.”</p>
  <p>Nothing here is automatically false, but the paragraph avoids the funding comparison. It could be pasted into dozens of education essays.</p>
  <p><strong>Prompt-locked version:</strong> “Removing university funding entirely would be short-sighted because economies also need doctors, engineers, and researchers whose training depends on degree-level study. Governments can expand vocational places for immediate shortages without treating every university course as a lower public priority.”</p>
  <p>The repaired version makes a claim about the exact decision, explains why, and keeps the position balanced.</p>

  <aside><p><strong>One-sentence warning:</strong> if a paragraph could answer a different question with only one noun changed, it is probably too generic.</p></aside>

  <h2>Stop overwriting with a paragraph budget</h2>
  <p>More words do not create a higher band by themselves. Use a job-based budget instead of chasing a perfect total:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> one clear paragraph claim.</li>
    <li><strong>Explanation:</strong> two or three sentences showing the mechanism or reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Example:</strong> one realistic illustration only when it strengthens the explanation.</li>
    <li><strong>Link back:</strong> a short consequence that reconnects to the task.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>When those four jobs are complete, stop. A second example often repeats the first or opens a new idea that you do not have time to develop.</p>

  <h2>Run the margin test while you practise</h2>
  <p>After writing each paragraph, place a short note in the margin: <em>What does this prove about the question?</em> Answer in eight words or fewer. If you cannot, underline the sentence where the paragraph drifted. Remove or rewrite from that point instead of polishing the vocabulary.</p>
  <p>Then review the full essay with three checks:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Does the introduction answer every instruction word?</li>
    <li>Does each body paragraph support the same position?</li>
    <li>Does the conclusion repeat the answer rather than introduce a rescue idea?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Final action: lock one prompt before writing</h2>
  <p>Open the <a href="/writing">IELTS Writing practice area</a> and choose one Task 2 question. Make the four-box prompt lock, then write only the two body-paragraph topic sentences. Use the <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-essay-structure-band-7">Band 7 essay structure guide</a> to place them in a complete response, and use the <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-coherence-cohesion-band-7">coherence and cohesion guide</a> to check how each explanation develops.</p>
  <p>Official format and scoring claims were checked on 21 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/test-types/ielts-academic-test/ielts-academic-format-writing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Academic Writing test format</a> and the British Council <a href="https://takeielts.britishcouncil.org/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-tips" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Writing Task 2 preparation guidance</a>.</p>
  $post$,
  'ielts-writing-task-2-stay-on-topic',
  ARRAY['IELTS Writing Task 2 off topic', 'how to stay on topic IELTS Writing', 'IELTS task response', 'avoid overwriting IELTS essay', 'IELTS question analysis', 'IELTS Writing planning'],
  'Writing', 6, '2026-07-21T08:00:00Z'::timestamptz, false, false, 'free',
  '/blog-images/ielts-writing-task-2-stay-on-topic.webp'
),
(
  'IELTS Reading Stuck at Band 6.5? Build a Better Error Log',
  'A diagnostic review method for IELTS Reading learners who keep repeating full tests but cannot see which decisions, question types, or habits cost marks.',
  'Break an IELTS Reading Band 6.5 plateau with a decision-based error log, targeted repair drills, and a weekly method for turning mistakes into progress.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-reading-band-6-5-error-log',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>You finish another Reading test, see almost the same score, and tell yourself to practise more. The next day you complete another full paper. The score moves by one mark, but you still cannot explain why the wrong answers were wrong.</p>
  <p>This pattern is common among learners around Band 6 or 6.5. A recent learner preparing for another attempt described getting stuck on difficult questions until time ran out. The useful shift is from collecting test scores to collecting evidence about the decisions that produce them.</p>

  <blockquote><p>An error log should not record only the correct answer. It should reveal the decision you need to make differently next time.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>Know what the score means—and what it does not</h2>
  <p>The Academic Reading test has 40 questions in 60 minutes, and each correct answer receives one mark. IELTS publishes average raw-score reference points, including about 23 correct answers for Band 6 and 30 for Band 7 in Academic Reading, while noting that exact boundaries can vary slightly by test version.</p>
  <p>That makes a plateau diagnostically useful. Moving from the low twenties toward 30 is not one giant reading skill. It is several recoverable marks: perhaps two lost to instruction errors, three to weak paraphrase recognition, and two to spending too long on uncertain evidence.</p>

  <h2>Replace the answer log with a decision log</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Log field</th><th scope="col">Weak note</th><th scope="col">Useful note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Question type</td><td>Reading</td><td>True / False / Not Given</td></tr>
      <tr><td>My decision</td><td>I guessed</td><td>I chose False because the passage did not repeat the claim</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Paragraph C</td><td>The passage discussed cost but never compared effectiveness</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Error cause</td><td>Careless</td><td>I treated missing information as a contradiction</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Next drill</td><td>Practise more</td><td>Classify 10 claims as contradiction or absent evidence</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The useful note names a repeatable choice. “Careless” is a feeling; “ignored the word limit” or “matched one repeated word without checking the whole sentence” is a repair target.</p>

  <h2>Sort every miss into five causes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Instruction:</strong> you exceeded the word limit, chose too many options, or transferred the wrong form.</li>
    <li><strong>Location:</strong> you searched the wrong paragraph or failed to track where the ordered questions had moved.</li>
    <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> you missed a paraphrase, contrast, reference word, or limiting expression.</li>
    <li><strong>Decision rule:</strong> you confused contradiction with absence, detail with main idea, or partial truth with the complete option.</li>
    <li><strong>Time and attention:</strong> you stayed too long, reread without a purpose, or carried panic into the next question.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Use only one primary cause per mistake. If every error receives three labels, the log becomes too vague to guide tomorrow's practice.</p>

  <h2>Use the 90-second forensic review</h2>
  <p>For each wrong answer, spend no more than 90 focused seconds on four steps:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the exact evidence or confirm that the information is absent.</li>
    <li>Underline the question phrase and the passage phrase that express the same or opposing meaning.</li>
    <li>Write the smallest rule that would have prevented the error.</li>
    <li>Create one mini-question that tests that rule again.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For example, if you chose an option because it repeated the word <em>benefit</em>, your rule might be: “Match the complete relationship, not one shared noun.” Your mini-question should contain two options that both mention the noun but differ in cause or degree.</p>

  <h2>Turn the weekly pattern into a drill</h2>
  <p>After two or three practice sets, count the primary causes. Do not attack all of them at once.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Pattern in the log</th><th scope="col">Next practice block</th><th scope="col">Evidence of improvement</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Four instruction errors</td><td>Untimed completion questions with a word-limit check before every answer</td><td>Two sets with zero format mistakes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Repeated paraphrase misses</td><td>Build question-to-passage phrase pairs from one article</td><td>Explain each pair without using the same wording</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Getting stuck on uncertain items</td><td>Use a 60–90 second stop-loss marker in a short timed set</td><td>Reach every question and return with time remaining</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One weak question type</td><td>Practise that type across several passages before another full test</td><td>Accuracy improves across two sources, not one memorised passage</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <aside><p><strong>Plateau rule:</strong> do not take another full test until you can name the pattern the previous test exposed and the drill you used to repair it.</p></aside>

  <h2>Keep official score boundaries in perspective</h2>
  <p>Raw-score references help you estimate the size of the gap, but they do not turn every practice paper into an official band prediction. Use reliable IELTS-format material, record the raw score, and look for a stable range across several controlled attempts. Your error categories are often more actionable than a single converted band.</p>

  <h2>Final action: review ten decisions</h2>
  <p>Open the <a href="/reading">IELTS Reading practice area</a> and complete one short set. Log every wrong or uncertain answer using the five fields, then choose only the most frequent cause for tomorrow's drill. Use the <a href="/blog/ielts-reading-true-false-not-given-strategy">True, False, Not Given evidence method</a> when contradiction and absence are mixed up, or the <a href="/blog/ielts-reading-time-management-computer-strategy">60-minute Reading strategy</a> when your log points to slow decisions.</p>
  <p>Official format and score claims were checked on 21 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/test-types/ielts-academic-test/ielts-academic-format-reading" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Academic Reading test format</a> and <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/your-results/ielts-scoring-in-detail" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">scoring guide</a>.</p>
  $post$,
  'ielts-reading-band-6-5-error-log',
  ARRAY['IELTS Reading stuck at Band 6.5', 'IELTS Reading error log', 'improve IELTS Reading score', 'IELTS Reading review mistakes', 'IELTS Reading Band 7', 'IELTS Reading diagnostic review'],
  'Reading', 6, '2026-07-21T09:00:00Z'::timestamptz, false, false, 'free',
  '/blog-images/ielts-reading-band-6-5-error-log.webp'
),
(
  'Missed an IELTS Listening Answer? Recover in 10 Seconds',
  'A calm recovery routine for IELTS Listening learners who miss one answer, keep thinking about it, and then lose the next part of the recording.',
  'Recover after a missed IELTS Listening answer with a 10-second reset, signpost tracking, and practice drills that stop one blank becoming several lost marks.',
  'https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-listening-missed-answer-recovery',
  'blog',
  $post$
  <p>You hear a possible answer, hesitate over the spelling, and realise the recording has moved on. Instead of following it, your mind keeps rebuilding the previous sentence. By the time you look at the next question, you have missed that one too.</p>
  <p>Many Listening losses happen in chains. The first blank may come from vocabulary or attention; the next two come from refusing to release it. Because official IELTS sample material plays each recording once, recovery is a skill you should practise deliberately rather than hope you will never need.</p>

  <blockquote><p>One missed answer costs one mark. Chasing it can cost the whole question group.</p></blockquote>

  <h2>Use the 10-second RELEASE reset</h2>
  <p>The name is longer than the action. Train the sequence until it feels automatic:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Step</th><th scope="col">Action</th><th scope="col">Reason</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>R — Record</td><td>Type or note the best fragment you genuinely heard, if any.</td><td>Preserves useful evidence without inventing an answer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>E — Exit</td><td>Mark the question and stop reconstructing it.</td><td>Prevents the previous item taking more attention.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>L — Locate</td><td>Look at the next unanswered question, not the missed one.</td><td>Gives your attention a new target.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>E — Expect</td><td>Identify one anchor: a name, topic change, option difference, or required word type.</td><td>Creates a signal you can listen for.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A — Attend</td><td>Listen for meaning around the anchor.</td><td>Rejoins the conversation or monologue.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>S/E — Stay / Evaluate later</td><td>Keep moving; revisit only when the recording or section permits.</td><td>Protects the remaining marks.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The reset may take less than 10 seconds once practised. The goal is not to count perfectly. It is to interrupt the panic loop before it follows you into the next answer.</p>

  <h2>Know the difference between a blank and being lost</h2>
  <p>A <strong>blank</strong> means you know where the audio is, but you did not catch one answer. Being <strong>lost</strong> means you no longer know which question the speakers are discussing. Use different responses:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>For one blank, move directly to the next question and listen for its anchor.</li>
    <li>When lost, scan two or three upcoming questions for a clear shift—a new speaker, place, stage, reason, or opinion—and rejoin there.</li>
    <li>Do not scan the entire page. Too much visual searching competes with listening.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Track signposts, not isolated keywords</h2>
  <p>The recording may mention words from several answer options. What matters is the speaker's complete meaning, especially when it changes.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Signpost</th><th scope="col">What may happen next</th><th scope="col">Listening response</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>But / however / actually</td><td>An earlier idea is corrected or limited.</td><td>Wait for the final position before choosing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>First / next / finally</td><td>The speaker moves through stages.</td><td>Use the sequence to relocate your question.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>So we decided / the main reason</td><td>A choice or cause is summarised.</td><td>Match the whole idea, not the first option mentioned.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Let's move on to</td><td>A topic boundary appears.</td><td>Release any previous blank and jump to the new group.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>A worked recovery moment</h2>
  <p>Suppose Question 16 asks why a student rejected the first research topic. You hear three possible problems but miss the final choice. Question 17 asks what topic the group will study instead.</p>
  <p>The risky move is to keep comparing the Question 16 options while the group announces the new topic. The recovery move is to mark 16, look at the key difference in the Question 17 options, and listen for a decision phrase such as <em>we'll focus on</em> or <em>we finally chose</em>. You may lose one mark, but you are positioned to catch 17 and the sequence after it.</p>

  <aside><p><strong>No backward listening:</strong> when the audio continues, your attention must move forward too. Review happens after the section, not inside the next sentence.</p></aside>

  <h2>Practise recovery on purpose</h2>
  <p>Normal practice rewards perfect concentration but does not test what happens after a mistake. Add one controlled disruption drill:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose an official-format Listening set and preview the questions normally.</li>
    <li>Before starting, choose one question to leave blank deliberately.</li>
    <li>At that question, perform RELEASE and relocate to the next anchor.</li>
    <li>After the audio, record how many seconds recovery took and whether the next two answers were protected.</li>
    <li>Repeat until the deliberate blank stays isolated.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Then remove the planned disruption and use the same routine only when needed. The drill teaches your nervous system that a blank is recoverable, not a reason to abandon the section.</p>

  <h2>Review the chain, not just the first miss</h2>
  <p>In your Listening error log, mark each wrong answer as either <em>original error</em> or <em>follow-on error</em>. If Question 12 was missed because of an unfamiliar paraphrase but Questions 13 and 14 were lost while thinking about 12, the primary training need is recovery. Without that distinction, you may spend hours learning vocabulary while the larger score leak remains attention control.</p>

  <h2>Final action: protect the next two answers</h2>
  <p>Open the <a href="/listening">IELTS Listening practice area</a> and complete one short set with a deliberate blank. Your target is not a perfect score; it is catching the next two answers cleanly. Use the <a href="/blog/improve-ielts-listening-band-6-to-7-plan">four-week Listening plan</a> to place the drill in a wider routine, and the <a href="/blog/ielts-listening-map-labelling-strategy">map-labelling strategy</a> when losing your place happens mainly during route descriptions.</p>
  <p>Official format and test-day claims were checked on 21 July 2026 against the IELTS <a href="https://www.ielts.org/take-a-test/preparation-resources/sample-test-questions/academic-test" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Academic sample test questions</a> and British Council <a href="https://takeielts.britishcouncil.org/blog/ielts-test-day-advice" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">test-day advice</a>.</p>
  $post$,
